{"id": "h-01", "question": "My local nursing home has 8 beds. Does it count as a hospital for my claim?", "strata": "definitional_carveout", "authored_by": "human", "expected_behaviour": "answer", "concept_id": "definition.hospital", "ground_truth_spans": [{"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 4, "clause_id": "def.hospital"}], "reference_answer": "Not on bed count alone, and the threshold depends on where it is. A Hospital must either be registered with the local authorities under the Clinical Establishments (Registration and Regulation) Act 2010 (or the enactments in the Schedule to Section 56(1)), or else comply with ALL of five minimum criteria: (i) qualified nursing staff under its employment round the clock; (ii) at least 10 in-patient beds in towns having a population of less than 10,00,000 and at least 15 in-patient beds in all other places; (iii) qualified medical practitioner(s) in charge round the clock; (iv) a fully equipped operation theatre of its own where surgical procedures are carried out; and (v) daily records of patients maintained and made accessible to the insurer's authorised personnel. So an 8-bed facility fails criterion (ii) wherever it is, unless it is separately registered under the Act.", "as_of_date": "2026-09-04", "notes": "Registration OR all five criteria - the 'or' matters and a retriever that returns only the criteria list misses the registration alternative. Bed count is itself population-gated (10 vs 15), both branches stated in the same sub-clause so no split risk. AYUSH Hospital is defined separately on p2 with entirely different criteria (government/teaching/registered standalone) - candidate for a later item, not chased."}
{"id": "h-02", "question": "I need surgery for a hernia. How long do I have to wait after buying the policy?", "strata": "definitional_carveout", "authored_by": "human", "expected_behaviour": "answer", "concept_id": "waiting_period.specified_disease", "ground_truth_spans": [{"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 31, "clause_id": "excl.02"}, {"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 32}], "reference_answer": "24 months. Hernia is a listed condition under the specified disease/procedure waiting period (item vi), and expenses for the listed conditions are excluded until the expiry of 24 months of continuous coverage after the date of inception of the first policy (Code Excl 02 A). The exclusion does not apply to claims arising due to an accident. On enhancement of Sum Insured it applies afresh to the increase (B), and if the condition also falls under the pre-existing disease waiting period then the longer of the two waiting periods applies (C).", "as_of_date": "2026-09-04", "notes": "Two spans. The excl.02 clause text (p31) states the 24-month period but never contains the word 'hernia'; the listed-conditions block that does is a separate region, so a retriever hitting only excl.02 cannot answer the question. The second span is p32 and NOT p31: p31's hernia occurrence at offset 402 is the tail of the RIDER-VARIANT list begun on p30 under 'Where the Insured Person has opted', while the standard list begins on p32. The block carries no clause identifier of its own (page_clauses[32] = ['3', 'II', 'excl.03']), so the span is page-keyed and the waiting_period.specified_disease anchors confirm p32."}
{"id": "h-03", "question": "I bought the policy five days ago and now I have a fever. Will the hospital bill be paid?", "strata": "definitional_carveout", "authored_by": "human", "expected_behaviour": "answer", "concept_id": "waiting_period.initial", "ground_truth_spans": [{"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 32, "clause_id": "excl.03"}], "reference_answer": "No, unless the fever results from an accident. Expenses related to the treatment of any illness within 30 days from the first policy commencement date are excluded except claims arising due to an accident (Code Excl 03 A). Two carve-outs follow: the exclusion does not apply if the Insured Person has Continuous Coverage for more than twelve months (B), and the waiting period applies afresh to any Sum Insured granted subsequently at a higher level (C).", "as_of_date": "2026-09-04", "notes": "Transcribed from p32: sub-clauses A (accident exception), B (continuous coverage over twelve months), C (applies afresh to enhanced Sum Insured)."}
{"id": "h-04", "question": "I missed my premium due date. How many days do I have to pay, and am I covered in the meantime?", "strata": "conditional_override", "authored_by": "human", "expected_behaviour": "answer", "concept_id": "definition.grace_period", "ground_truth_spans": [{"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 42, "clause_id": "12"}, {"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 41, "clause_id": "9"}], "reference_answer": "It depends on how you pay, and the two clauses say opposite things about cover. If you pay by instalment (clause 12, p42): the grace period is 15 days for monthly and 30 days for quarterly, half-yearly or yearly instalments, and coverage IS available during that grace period (item viii). If the instalment is not received within it, the policy is cancelled (item v). If you are at the end of the policy period and renewing (clause 9, p41): the policy terminates and can be renewed within a grace period of 30 days, but coverage is NOT available during that grace period (item ii). In both cases, paying within the grace period protects the accrued credits - sum insured, no-claim bonus, specific waiting periods, pre-existing disease waiting period and moratorium.", "as_of_date": "2026-09-04", "notes": "Third conditional override found by labelling, and the sharpest: p41 cl.9 says 'coverage is not available during the Grace Period' while p42 cl.12 item viii says 'coverage is available during the Grace Period also' for instalment payers. Flatly opposite wording, both correct, gated on payment mode. A retriever that hits either page alone answers confidently and wrongly for half of all users."}
