{"id": "h-28", "question": "I have diabetes and declared it when I bought the policy. How long until my diabetes treatment is covered?", "strata": "conditional_override", "authored_by": "human", "expected_behaviour": "answer", "concept_id": "waiting_period.pre_existing_disease", "ground_truth_spans": [{"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 31, "clause_id": "excl.01"}, {"document_id": "star-comprehensive-2025", "page": 30, "clause_id": "25"}], "reference_answer": "36 months of continuous coverage from the date of inception of the first policy, unless you bought the optional buy-back cover. Expenses for a pre-existing disease and its direct complications are excluded until the expiry of 36 months (Code Excl 01 A). The 12-month figure elsewhere in the wording is the Optional Cover (Buy Back of Pre-Existing Disease Waiting Period), which applies only on payment of additional premium, only if elected at first purchase, and subject to pre-acceptance medical screening - so it does not apply by default. Two further conditions apply either way: on enhancement of Sum Insured the exclusion applies afresh to the increase (B), and cover after 36 months requires the disease to have been declared at application and accepted by the insurer (D).", "as_of_date": "2026-09-04", "notes": "Conditional override. p31 excl.01 = 36 months (policy-wide). p30 = 12 months but inside the rider-variant block gated on clause 25 'Optional Cover (Buy Back of PED Waiting Period)'. Both figures correct; the gate is closed by default. Per DESIGN R1 the answer must SURFACE the gate, not pick a branch - an answer of '12 months' without the condition is the failure this item exists to catch."}
